# The module's capstone is the one exercise here whose working storage has to
# fit the CALLER'S data -- which is why its `allowed` list is the only one
# besides create_node/insert_data to carry malloc and free. A breadth-first
# walk has to remember the nodes it has reached but not yet visited, and room
# of a fixed size is the cheapest thing to reach for.
#
# Nothing else in this repo could contradict that guess, once. Measured on a
# walk that kept its waiting nodes in room for 64: the fixture's hand-wired
# trees reproduced BYTE FOR BYTE, clean under ASan+UBSan on their shapes, the
# 400k differential OK and the 200k crash-fuzz OK -- those trees were 6 nodes
# at most and the corpus was capped twice over (10 items drawn, 64 decoded).
# The corpus now holds trees of 600 and 2000 items (finding 041), which room
# for 64 does not survive, and a capacity past those still does. The valgrind
# layer is no help either, because room of a fixed size is normally on the
# stack, which memcheck does not see past (valgrind_test.sh's OK line says
# so).
#
# So the size of a tree is an axis no layer measured, and two now do. The
# output fixture ends on a HEAP-SHAPED tree of 20000 nodes (fifteen levels,
# 8192 on the widest), checked by construction and shown as one line: every
# reading of the subject agrees on that walk, so it is a basic case. And this
# probe measures both dimensions under ASan: the same 20000-node tree, and a
# 5000-deep SPINE, handed over with every node and every item allocated to its
# exact size. The two are not redundant -- each catches a variant the other
# misses. The heap tree reds room for the waiting nodes fixed at a round
# number, and the spine does not (a spine is one node per level, so it is never
# wide). The spine reds a variant with room enough for its waiting nodes but a
# fixed number of something it keeps per LEVEL, and the heap tree does not,
# because it is only fifteen levels deep. The first version used a complete
# tree of 1023 nodes and a 200-deep spine and said it failed a capacity chosen
# as a constant at any size -- and room for 1024 passed it (finding 041); the
# sizes now sit past every power of two a guess is likely to stop at. Width and
# depth both come from the caller's data, so the probe fails a capacity chosen
# as a constant on either axis.
#
# It asserts nothing about the OUTPUT -- visit order, level numbers and
# is_first_elem belong to the *_output and *_diff layers, and a second red
# saying the same thing would only be noise. See the probe's own header for
# what each shape is aimed at.
c_mem_check(
    num = "07",
    fn = "btree_apply_by_level",
    probe = "mem_btree_apply_by_level.c",
)


# ---------------------------------------------------------------- cost
# Same unit, same reason, as c-12: every other c_cycles target measures per
# BYTE over a hex field, and this corpus encodes a STRUCTURE -- the insertion
# sequence a tree is built from, comma-joined. A per-byte figure over "61,62,63"
# would measure the corpus's decimal formatting, not the traversal.
#
# The unit is the NODE, counted off that field: its number of comma-separated
# items, which split() returns (0 for an empty field): c-12's ELEM.
#
# No instruction budget: a budget is a MEASURED number, and the only honest way
# to get one is to measure a correct implementation of this exercise, which does
# not exist yet. The per-node figure is reported meanwhile.
#
# syscall_budget = 0 is assertable from the subject and is the strongest thing
# these targets can say: a traversal applies a function, it does not print.
# btree_apply_* take the function to call as an argument, which is precisely the
# subject saying the printing is not theirs to do.
NODE = "split($1,a,/,/)"

# The corpus's trees of 600 and 2000 nodes test capacity (finding 041; the
# diff layers judge them) and are left out of every figure here, as C 12's
# long lists are.
LONG_CASES = 500

c_cycles(
    num = "01",
    fn = "btree_apply_prefix",
